<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; Chapter 3 &gt; Level 10</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 3 Level 10 Walkthrough: Garden</h1>

    <p>Garden is room 10 of Chapter 3, <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and it is the chapter's daytime room. After the dusk of Backyard and the indoor clutter of Kids Playroom, this one is bright and open: a lawn, a raised deck, a shed, and a stone wall covered in pink flowering vine. It is also the first Chapter 3 room where the main lock is a letter lock rather than a keypad, and the answer is not a word — it is letters sorted by the shape of their letterforms.</p>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Rebuilt from the level's full recording, sampled at one frame per second across all <b>226 seconds (3:46)</b>. The letter lock <b>B · N · U · G</b> was read on the full frame at 1:49 and again from a 3x crop of the same frame; the second code, <b>8 5 2 7</b>, was read on the full frame at 3:24 and again on a 2.4x crop of the bench, and then on the padlock itself at 3:37 on both the full frame and a 2x crop. The herb tags, the 1911 notice header, the pattern-tool instruction, the brick-wall instruction and the cup-handle line were each read whole-frame and re-read on crops. Anything still open is listed at the bottom under <i>What this page could not verify</i>.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={3} current={10} variant="jump" label="Chapter 3 · Murder Dressed in White" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>Garden is room 10 of 15 in Chapter 3 and it asks for <b>two</b> codes. The four-dial letter lock takes <b>B · N · U · G</b>, top to bottom, and it is not a word — the clue is the pattern tool's <b>shape order</b>. The shed padlock takes <b>8 5 2 7</b>, painted on the bench slats.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Read the three-tier planter by the shed: <b>Chives</b> and <b>Thyme</b> on top, <b>Basil</b> and <b>Carrots</b> in the middle. Take the <b>Key</b> at 0:10 and the <b>Trowel</b> at 0:32.</li>
        <li>Open the dated journal — its header reads <b>1911</b> and one bold line on it reads <b>THE SHAPE ORDER</b>.</li>
        <li>Open the pattern tool. The instruction reads <b>ARRANGE THE LETTERS THAT APPEAR IN THE PATTERN TOOL BASED ON THE SHAPE ORDER</b>.</li>
        <li>Set the four vertical dials to <b>B</b>, <b>N</b>, <b>U</b>, <b>G</b>. The fourth dial turns through W and K before it lands on G, so do not stop on a letter that merely looks plausible.</li>
        <li>Work the brick wall: <b>MAKE THE LARGE BRICK ON TOP STICK OUT, THEN TOUCH IT</b> — the wall then shows the brick it wants, and touching that one dismantles it.</li>
        <li>Clear the round cover in the lawn by running its maze, then turn the four red-capped valves under it to match the <b>four coffee-cup handles</b> on the tray.</li>
        <li>Take <b>8 5 2 7</b> off the bench slats, set it on the shed's four-wheel padlock, and walk through the shed's exit icon.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
      <div class="yt-facade" data-yt="M1CT8F7yG9I" data-title="Rooms &amp; Exits Level 10 Garden Walkthrough Murder Dressed in White Chapter 3"
           role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Rooms &amp; Exits Level 10 Garden Walkthrough">
        <img src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/M1CT8F7yG9I/maxresdefault.jpg"
             alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 10 Garden walkthrough video" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1280" height="720"
             onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='https://i.ytimg.com/vi/M1CT8F7yG9I/hqdefault.jpg'">
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="video-cap">The run used for every frame on this page.</p>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-hero-garden.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 10 Garden: a raised wooden deck with a light blue outdoor sofa set under a beige umbrella, a wooden garden shed on the left, a tiered pallet planter, and a stone wall covered in pink flowering vines" width="1200" height="540" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span>.</h2>
    <p>A working garden on a bright day. The lawn is closely mown and cut across by large pale stepping stones that run from the lower corner up to a raised wooden deck. On the deck sits a light wicker sofa set with pale blue cushions and darker blue throw pillows, arranged around a low coffee table under a square beige umbrella. A metal and glass lantern sits at the edge of the decking.</p>
    <p>To the left stands a small garden shed, timber-planked with a terracotta-tiled gable and a plain door with a silver handle. Beside it is the most interactive object in the room: a three-tier planter built from stacked pallets, each tier carrying a black chalkboard tag with a plant name. A square planter with a large-leaved plant stands next to it. Along the back runs a low dry-stone wall, heavily covered with a vine of small bright pink flowers — this is the wall the brick puzzle works on. A cypress, a leafy tree and a gnarled smaller tree break up the skyline; a wooden bench with dark metal legs sits on the lawn, and a small wooden chest rests by the back wall.</p>
    <p>Design-wise this room is looser than its neighbours. Two codes carry it — the four-dial letter device on the ledge and the four-wheel padlock on the shed — and between them sits a lawn chain: the mower, the mazed cover, the four-valve manifold, and the tray of cups that unlocks it. That makes it faster than Backyard but easier to stall in, because if you do not read the pattern-tool instruction carefully you will keep trying to spell a word on a lock that does not want one, and if you never look down at the bench you will reach the shed with no code at all.</p>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-herb-labels.jpg" alt="The three-tier pallet planter in Garden with chalkboard labels reading Chives, Thyme, Basil and Carrots" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />

    <h2>The map: what is where<span class="accent">.</span>.</h2>
    <p>Every interactive object we could place, and what it is for.</p>
    <table class="item-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Where</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Three-tier pallet planter</td><td>Left, beside the shed</td><td>Chalkboard tags: <b>Chives</b>, <b>Thyme</b> (top), <b>Basil</b>, <b>Carrots</b> (middle). Identifies the plants.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Garden shed</td><td>Far left</td><td>Timber, terracotta gable, single door with a silver handle.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Raised deck</td><td>Centre-left</td><td>Sofa set with blue cushions, coffee table, beige umbrella, lantern.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pattern tool</td><td>Opened from the bag / a panel</td><td>Hexagonal honeycomb pattern with the instruction <b>ARRANGE THE LETTERS THAT APPEAR IN THE PATTERN TOOL BASED ON THE SHAPE ORDER</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Four-dial letter lock</td><td>On a wooden ledge</td><td>Vertical dials. Answer <b>B N U G</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dry-stone wall</td><td>Back boundary, under pink vine</td><td>Brick puzzle: <b>MAKE THE LARGE BRICK ON TOP STICK OUT, THEN TOUCH IT</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dated journal</td><td>Opened from the patio side</td><td>Two-page printed sheet with <b>1911</b> in the header and a bold line reading <b>THE SHAPE ORDER</b>; the room's document clue.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Round lawn cover</td><td>Centre of the lawn, set flush</td><td>Circular plate with a maze in concentric bands and a white orb; trace the orb to clear it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Four-valve manifold</td><td>In the pit under the cover</td><td>H-shaped pipe run with four red handwheels; set them from the cup handles.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tray of four cups</td><td>Outdoor table, shown 3:10</td><td>The clue <b>PAY ATTENTION TO THE POSITION OF THE GLASS HANDLE</b> — four handles, four valves.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Key</td><td>Carried, from 0:10</td><td>The room's first pickup; never shown being used.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Trowel</td><td>Carried, from 0:32</td><td>Digging tool for the beds and planter tiers.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shears</td><td>Carried, around 1:52</td><td>Cutting tool; the footage does not show the cut.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rope</td><td>Patio side, 2:23</td><td>Combined with the <b>Handle</b> at 2:29 into <b>Rope with handle</b>; never used on screen.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shed padlock</td><td>Shed door, with a sliding bolt</td><td>Four wheels, starts at <b>0 0 0 0</b>, takes <b>8 5 2 7</b>. Opening it is the room's exit route.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wooden bench</td><td>Lawn, by the stepping stones</td><td>Its seat slats carry <b>8 5 2 7</b> in pale grey-blue paint.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wooden chest</td><td>Back wall, on the grass</td><td>Small and unlocked-looking; not opened in our footage.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span>.</h2>
    <p>The order below follows the recording. Timestamps are video time; the links jump to the matching second.</p>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:05</span>
      <strong>Sweep the garden from the deck</strong>
      The upload opens on a loading screen and a keyhole vignette, and by 0:05 the whole space is on screen. Read it in one pass: shed and planter on the left, deck and sofa in the middle, lawn with stepping stones running to the bench, and the flowering wall closing the back. Almost everything interactive is visible from that one angle, which is why this room plays faster than Backyard.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-hero-garden.jpg" alt="The Garden at the start of Level 10, with the deck, shed, planter and flowering stone wall in view" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=5s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:05</a></div>
    </div>
    <InContentAd />

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:08 · the planter</span>
      <strong>Chives, Thyme, Basil, Carrots</strong>
      The first thing worth reading is the pallet planter beside the shed. The panel opens on it at 0:08 and the tags fill the screen by 0:13: three tiers of stacked crates, each with a black chalkboard tag — <b>Chives</b> and <b>Thyme</b> on the top tier, <b>Basil</b> and <b>Carrots</b> on the middle one, and unlabelled plants on the bottom. The tags are the room telling you which plant is which before it starts asking you to cut or pull one. These names are worth writing down — the tags, not the plants.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-herb-labels.jpg" alt="The three-tier planter in Garden with chalkboard tags for Chives, Thyme, Basil and Carrots" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=8s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:08</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:10 and 0:32 · key and trowel</span>
      <strong>The first two tools</strong>
      A <b>Key</b> is collected almost immediately, at 0:10, and the <b>Trowel</b> follows at 0:32. Neither is a code; both go straight into the bag. The key never gets a visible lock of its own in this run, and the trowel is never seen in the soil — they are pickups, and the room moves on. Still, take them: nothing you pick up here is scenery.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-trowel.jpg" alt="The trowel being collected in the Garden level" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=10s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:10</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=32s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:32</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 0:46 · the journal</span>
      <strong>A printed page dated 1911</strong>
      A document opens full screen: an open journal, two printed pages, dated <b>1911</b> in the header, with the body set in small type across both pages. One bold line sits across the spread and is the only part that reads cleanly: <b>THE SHAPE ORDER</b>. That is the phrase the pattern tool turns into a full instruction twenty seconds later, so the journal is the room's first hint rather than decoration. The rest of the body is too small to read at this resolution — read it in your own copy.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-1911-notice.jpg" alt="A printed notice dated 1911 opened full screen in the Garden level" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=46s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:46</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 1:07 · the pattern tool</span>
      <strong>ARRANGE THE LETTERS THAT APPEAR IN THE PATTERN TOOL BASED ON THE SHAPE ORDER</strong>
      The key instruction of the whole room. The pattern tool fills the screen with a dark hexagonal honeycomb, and the line across the bottom tells you what the lock wants: letters from the pattern, arranged by <b>shape order</b>. Not spelling, not alphabetical, not the order you found them. This is the sentence that stops people in this level, because it reads like a word puzzle and is not one.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-pattern-tool-letters.jpg" alt="The pattern tool overlay in Garden with the instruction to arrange letters based on shape order" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=67s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:07</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 1:24 to 1:49 · the lock</span>
      <strong>B · N · U · G</strong>
      The lock is a dark metal block with four vertical letter dials. In the recording the first three are already set — <b>B</b>, <b>N</b>, <b>U</b> — and the fourth is being turned: it passes through W, then K, then U again, before it lands on <b>G</b> at 1:49 and the panel closes. That is the answer: <b>B N U G</b>, top to bottom, and yes, it looks wrong. It is the shape order, not a word.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-dial-lock-bnug.jpg" alt="The four-dial letter lock in Garden set to B, N, U and G top to bottom" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=109s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:49</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 1:52 · the shears</span>
      <strong>Third tool in the chain</strong>
      <b>Shears</b> come into the bag a few seconds after the letter lock opens, which is the room's rhythm: lock, tool, ground, lock. The recording shows them collected and then moves straight on to the wall, so this page does not claim a target for them. They are the cutting tool in a room full of vines and beds; what they actually cut is not on screen.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-shears.jpg" alt="The shears being collected in the Garden level" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=112s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:52</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 2:01 · the wall</span>
      <strong>MAKE THE LARGE BRICK ON TOP STICK OUT</strong>
      The stone wall under the flowering vine carries the room's most explicit instruction, printed across the middle of the screen: <b>MAKE THE LARGE BRICK ON TOP STICK OUT, THEN TOUCH IT. THE REQUIRED BRICK WILL APPEAR, TOUCH IT TO DISMANTLE IT.</b> Push a brick proud of the face and touch it; the wall answers by showing you the one it actually wants. Touch that and it comes away. No numbers, no order to memorise — just the correct brick, twice.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-brick-puzzle.jpg" alt="The dry-stone wall in Garden under a pink flowering vine, with the brick dismantle instruction on screen" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=121s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:01</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 2:23 · the rope</span>
      <strong>Rope, then Rope with handle</strong>
      A <b>Rope</b> comes off the patio end of the room at 2:23. The bag is opened at 2:27 and holds two separate items — <b>Rope</b> and <b>Handle</b> — with <i>Use / Combine / Dismantle / Examine</i> across the bottom of the panel; <b>Combine</b> at 2:29 leaves a single item labelled <b>Rope with handle</b>. That is the last thing the inventory does in this recording. It never opens again, so whatever the rope with handle is for, this run does not show it.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-rope.jpg" alt="The Rope item collected in Garden, shown full screen with its name underneath" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-rope-handle.jpg" alt="The Garden inventory after combining Rope and Handle, showing the single item labelled Rope with handle" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=143s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:23</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=149s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:29</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 2:33 · the mower</span>
      <strong>The one lawn job in the room</strong>
      The camera drops out onto the grass and holds on a red push mower: red deck, four black wheels, a black engine block with a grey air filter, and a silver U-shaped handlebar. Nothing is attached to it — no cord, no rope, no grip — and it is never touched on screen. What it explains is the lawn itself: the wide shots show a paler, shorter strip where the mower has been, and the round plate sits in the grass beyond it.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-mower.jpg" alt="The red push lawnmower on the Garden lawn, with its black engine and silver handlebar" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=153s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:33</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 11 · 2:44 · the round cover</span>
      <strong>A maze cut into a plate in the lawn</strong>
      A circular plate sits flush in the grass, ringed by a raised lip. Cut into its face is a maze in concentric bands: walls, gaps, and a small white orb sitting in one channel. This is not a lid to lift — you steer the orb through the maze. Tracing it is what clears the plate: by 3:04 the plate is gone and the pit underneath is open.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-cover-maze.jpg" alt="The circular lawn cover in Garden with a maze in concentric bands and a small white orb in one channel" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=164s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:44</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 12 · 3:04 · the manifold</span>
      <strong>Four red handwheels on an H-shaped pipe run</strong>
      With the plate off, the pit shows an H-shaped manifold: two horizontal pipe runs joined by a cross-piece, each end capped with a red handwheel. Four caps, four locks. In the first frames after the cover clears, all four point the same way — that is the starting position, not the answer.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-valve-manifold.jpg" alt="The four-valve pipe manifold in the pit under the Garden lawn cover, four red handwheels on an H-shaped run" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=184s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:04</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 13 · 3:10 · the four cups</span>
      <strong>PAY ATTENTION TO THE POSITION OF THE GLASS HANDLE</strong>
      The instruction appears across the screen at 3:10 and the camera sits on a tray holding <b>four cups on saucers</b> under a beige umbrella. Handles, not cups. Four of them, which is the only count that matters here — the manifold under the lawn has exactly four caps, and this tray is the only four-way clue in the room.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-cup-hint.jpg" alt="The tray of four coffee cups in Garden under the instruction PAY ATTENTION TO THE POSITION OF THE GLASS HANDLE" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=190s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:10</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 14 · 3:20 · setting the valves</span>
      <strong>Copy the handles, one cap at a time</strong>
      Back at the pit from 3:14, the caps are turned one at a time — the frames show them going from all-alike to a mixed arrangement by 3:21. The instruction only reads one way: each cap repeats the direction of one cup handle. There is no number and no order to memorise; the tray is the whole solution. (Our 720p frames are too soft to tabulate which cup points where — see the notes at the bottom.)
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-valves-set.jpg" alt="The four-valve manifold in Garden with the red handwheels turned into a mixed arrangement" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=200s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:20</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 15 · 3:24 · 8 5 2 7</span>
      <strong>The second code is painted on the bench</strong>
      The camera pushes in on the slatted wooden bench by the stepping stones and holds on its seat. Painted across the slats in pale grey-blue are four digits: <b>8 · 5 · 2 · 7</b>. They are not scratched in or stencilled — they are brushed on, with drips at the edges, and they read the same on the full frame and on a 2.4x crop of it.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-code-8527.jpg" alt="The four digits 8527 painted in pale paint across the slats of the wooden bench in Garden" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=204s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:24</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 16 · 3:28 · the shed door</span>
      <strong>A four-wheel padlock sitting on 0 0 0 0</strong>
      The camera swings to the shed. The door is shut, and the hardware on it is two pieces: a four-wheel combination padlock hanging on the hasp, and a separate sliding bolt below it. The wheels start clean at <b>0 0 0 0</b>, so there is no partial code to read off the lock itself — everything you need is on the bench.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-padlock.jpg" alt="The four-wheel combination padlock on the Garden shed door, its wheels set to 0000" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=208s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:28</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 17 · 3:37 · the padlock opens</span>
      <strong>8 · 5 · 2 · 7 on the dials</strong>
      The wheels turn from 3:29. The last one spins through 0, 1, 2 and keeps going while the camera holds, and at 3:37 the window reads <b>8 5 2 7</b> — the bench number, character for character. Both the full frame and a 2x crop of that frame were read for these four digits and both gave the same answer. The camera pulls back a moment later and the next shot of the door has no padlock on it at all, so the lock comes off on this combination.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-padlock-8527.jpg" alt="The Garden shed padlock with its four wheels settled on 8527" width="1200" height="540" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=217s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:37</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 18 · 3:41 · the way out</span>
      <strong>Through the shed</strong>
      With the padlock off and the bolt drawn, the shed door swings open and a white exit icon — a figure walking through a doorway — stands in the dark of the shed. That is the room's exit; there is nothing behind it but the door. The recording cuts to a <i>Thanks for watching</i> card at 3:44 with no completion panel, no star tally and no coin award: the counter sits at 1,540 from the first frame to the last.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l10-shed-exit.jpg" alt="The Garden shed door standing open with the white exit icon of a figure walking through a doorway" width="1200" height="673" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=M1CT8F7yG9I&t=221s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:41</a></div>
    </div>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span>.</h2>
    <p><b>The letter lock will not accept a word.</b> It is not supposed to. The instruction says <b>shape order</b>, and the answer is <b>B N U G</b>. Every instinct you have about four-letter locks will fight this; ignore it.</p>
    <p><b>The fourth dial keeps slipping past the right letter.</b> In the recording it goes W, K, U and then G. The dials are small and the letters are close together, so turn slowly and watch the window rather than the wheel.</p>
    <p><b>There is a second code, and it is not on the shed.</b> The padlock is the last thing people try and the first thing they give up on, because nothing near it carries digits. The number is painted along the seat of the bench on the lawn — walk the lawn before you walk to the door.</p>
    <p><b>The brick will not come out.</b> Two touches, in order: first the brick you make stick out, then the one the wall offers you. Touching the offered brick first does nothing, and pushing a different brick proud gives you a different offer.</p>
    <p><b>The herb tags look decorative.</b> They are the only place the plant names exist in the room, and they are worth reading once — Chives and Thyme on top, Basil and Carrots in the middle. This run never asks you to name a plant, so treat them as the room's labelling rather than as a code you have to solve.</p>

    <h2>Where Garden sits in Chapter 3<span class="accent">.</span>.</h2>
    <p>Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and Garden is room <b>10 of 15</b>. It follows Backyard and comes two thirds of the way through the chapter. The run of three rooms — Kids Playroom, Backyard, Garden — is where Chapter 3 stops being a series of interiors and starts using larger, more open spaces, and the puzzle design follows: fewer enforced sequences, more scenery that turns out to be interactive, and codes that sit on the scenery rather than on a panel you walk up to.</p>
    <p>Carry one habit out of Garden: read the instruction text before touching anything. This room punishes assumptions harder than the two before it, and the whole letter-lock dead end exists because the instruction is easy to skim.</p>

