    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span>.</h2>
    <div class="warn">
      <b>1. The body text of the 1911 document.</b> The header reads 1911 in every frame we have, and one bold line on the same screen — <b>THE SHAPE ORDER</b> — is legible at full confidence on two separate crops. The paragraph around it is printed too small to transcribe at 720p, so it is not quoted here. If the page carries a second instruction in that body, this page does not have it.
      <br><br>
      <b>2. What the Key and the Shears open.</b> Both enter the bag (0:10 and 1:52) and neither is seen being used. The Rope is now covered — it becomes <b>Rope with handle</b> at 2:29 — but the video never shows the combined item being used either. All three are listed as collected, not as steps with targets.
      <br><br>
      <b>3. The four cup handles, valve by valve.</b> The line <b>PAY ATTENTION TO THE POSITION OF THE GLASS HANDLE</b> is read at full confidence, and the four valves are visibly turned between 3:14 and 3:21. But at 720p each cup handle is only a few pixels wide and two crops of the same tray gave different readings, so this page does not print a per-valve setting. Copy the handles from your own copy of the room; the count (four cups, four caps) is the part we are sure of.
      <br><br>
      <b>4. What the valve manifold unlocks.</b> The caps turn, the camera pulls away, and the garden looks the same as it did before — no gate opens, no object appears, nothing new is on the lawn. The manifold is described here as a solved lock with no visible consequence, because that is all the run shows.
      <br><br>
      <b>5. Whether the brick wall was actually dismantled.</b> The instruction is on screen from 2:01 to 2:10 and the camera studies the wall, but no brick comes away in the frames and the run never returns to that wall. The wording is quoted because it is printed; the solution is not shown on this page because it is not shown in the video.
      <br><br>
      <b>6. Any completion panel or reward.</b> The recording ends on a <i>Thanks for watching</i> card from 3:43. The coin counter reads 1,540 in every frame we read, from 0:03 to 3:42, and no star, score or payout screen appears, so there is nothing to report for the level's reward.
